Downgrading to 1.34.0 presented me with the following system extensions warning on startup;
<img width="262" alt="Screenshot 2023-02-01 at 16 48 24" src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/168870/216108691-11cb6fe5-c58c-4340-88fc-45d771ce0baf.png">


After I resolved the issue in System Preferences everything worked as expected.

Thanks. That's showing that a different VPN interface (`utun0`) is the default route in a lot of cases:

```
21:18:37 | diag: routetable: {Family: IPv4, Dst: 0.0.0.0/5, Interface: utun0, Sys: {GatewayInterface: utun0, GatewayIdx: 15, Flags: [global up {RTF_PRCLONING}]}}
21:18:37 | diag: routetable: {Family: IPv4, Dst: 0.0.0.0/0, Gateway: 192.168.138.1, Interface: en0, Sys: {Flags: [gateway global static up {RTF_PRCLONING}]}}
21:18:37 | diag: routetable: {Family: IPv4, Dst: 0.0.0.0/0, Interface: utun0, Sys: {GatewayInterface: utun0, GatewayIdx: 15, Flags: [global ifscope static up {RTF_CLONING}]}}
```

Do you have any other VPN software enabled? If possible, could you check in the "Network" section of the macOS system report (accessible from the About This Mac window) and see if it reports a service active with the BSD device name being `utun0`? Here's an example of what I see:

<img width="1015" alt="image" src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/513813/219457642-3deac4ab-0ef6-4377-8671-c2940725a32e.png">

@reporter If I may, a possible troubleshooting step here. Install Little Snitch (https://www.obdev.at/products/littlesnitch/index.html) and run it in free trial mode to see what incoming and outgoing connection activity can tell you. Run it in 'Observe' mode while you try other troubleshooting steps. The Network Monitor will show you a live view of connection activity.
Best of luck! 
Oh, one other thing, you might try flushing DNS as well, in case there’s a holdover from running NextDNS. ¯\_(ツ)_/¯
